# 4. Create the Web Application

Create a folder:

```text
kubernetes-web-app
```

Inside:

```text
kubernetes-web-app/
│
├── Dockerfile
├── index.html
├── deployment.yaml
└── service.yaml
```

---

# 5. Create `index.html`

Create:

```text
index.html
```

Add:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Kubernetes Web Application</title>
</head>

<body>

    <h1>Welcome to Kubernetes!</h1>

    <p>
        This web application is deployed and managed using Kubernetes.
    </p>

    <p>
        Version: 1.0
    </p>

</body>

</html>
```

---

# 6. Create the Dockerfile

Create:

```text
Dockerfile
```

Add:

```dockerfile
FROM nginx:alpine

COPY index.html /usr/share/nginx/html/index.html

EXPOSE 80
```

This creates an Nginx-based Docker image containing our web application.

---

# 7. Build the Docker Image

Open a terminal in the project directory.

Run:

```bash
docker build -t kubernetes-web-app:v1 .
```

Check the image:

```bash
docker images
```

You should see:

```text
REPOSITORY             TAG
kubernetes-web-app    v1
```

---

# 8. Create the Kubernetes Deployment

Create:

```text
deployment.yaml
```

Add:

```yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment

metadata:
  name: web-app-deployment

spec:
  replicas: 3

  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: web-app

  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: web-app

    spec:
      containers:
        - name: web-container
          image: kubernetes-web-app:v1
          imagePullPolicy: IfNotPresent

          ports:
            - containerPort: 80
```

---

# 9. Understand the Deployment

### Deployment name

```yaml
name: web-app-deployment
```

The Kubernetes Deployment is called:

```text
web-app-deployment
```

### Three replicas

```yaml
replicas: 3
```

Kubernetes will maintain:

```text
3 Pods
```

So:

```text
Deployment
    │
    ├── Pod 1
    ├── Pod 2
    └── Pod 3
```

### Docker image

```yaml
image: kubernetes-web-app:v1
```

Each Pod runs our Docker image.

---

# 10. Deploy the Application

Run:

```bash
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
```

Expected:

```text
deployment.apps/web-app-deployment created
```

---

# 11. Check the Deployment

Run:

```bash
kubectl get deployments
```

Expected:

```text
NAME                 READY   UP-TO-DATE   AVAILABLE
web-app-deployment   3/3     3            3
```

The important value is:

```text
3/3
```

This means all three desired Pods are ready.

---

# 12. Check the Pods

Run:

```bash
kubectl get pods
```

You should see three Pods:

```text
NAME                                  READY   STATUS    RESTARTS
web-app-deployment-xxxxx-xxxxx        1/1     Running   0
web-app-deployment-xxxxx-yyyyy        1/1     Running   0
web-app-deployment-xxxxx-zzzzz        1/1     Running   0
```

The actual names will be different on your computer.

---

# 13. Create a Kubernetes Service

Pods are temporary, so we normally don't access them directly.

Create a Service to provide a stable endpoint.

Create:

```text
service.yaml
```

Add:

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service

metadata:
  name: web-app-service

spec:
  type: NodePort

  selector:
    app: web-app

  ports:
    - protocol: TCP
      port: 80
      targetPort: 80
      nodePort: 30080
```

---

# 14. Understand the Service

The Service connects the browser to the Pods:

```text
             Service
                │
       ┌────────┼────────┐
       ▼        ▼        ▼
     Pod 1    Pod 2    Pod 3
       │        │        │
       └────────┴────────┘
                │
           Web Application
```

The important part is:

```yaml
selector:
  app: web-app
```

The Service sends traffic to Pods having:

```text
app=web-app
```

---

# 15. Create the Service

Run:

```bash
kubectl apply -f service.yaml
```

Expected:

```text
service/web-app-service created
```

Check:

```bash
kubectl get services
```

You should see:

```text
NAME              TYPE       CLUSTER-IP    PORT(S)
web-app-service   NodePort   10.x.x.x      80:30080/TCP
```

---

# 16. Open the Web Application

For Docker Desktop Kubernetes, try:

```text
http://localhost:30080
```

You should see:

```text
Welcome to Kubernetes!

This web application is deployed and managed using Kubernetes.

Version: 1.0
```

Your application is now deployed.

