# 18. Scale the Application

Suppose traffic increases.

Currently:

```text
3 Pods
```

Scale to 5:

```bash
kubectl scale deployment web-app-deployment --replicas=5
```

Check:

```bash
kubectl get pods
```

You should now have:

```text
5 Running Pods
```

Check:

```bash
kubectl get deployment
```

Expected:

```text
NAME                 READY
web-app-deployment   5/5
```

---

# 19. Scale Down

You can reduce the number of Pods:

```bash
kubectl scale deployment web-app-deployment --replicas=2
```

Check:

```bash
kubectl get pods
```

Now Kubernetes should maintain two Pods.

---

# 20. Test Kubernetes Self-Healing

This is an important demonstration.

First:

```bash
kubectl get pods
```

Suppose you have:

```text
Pod A
Pod B
```

Delete one:

```bash
kubectl delete pod POD_NAME
```

For example:

```bash
kubectl delete pod web-app-deployment-xxxxx-xxxxx
```

Immediately check:

```bash
kubectl get pods
```

You may temporarily see the deleted Pod terminating and a new Pod being created.

After a few seconds:

```text
2/2 Pods Running
```

### Why?

The Deployment has:

```yaml
replicas: 2
```

Kubernetes automatically creates a replacement to maintain the desired state.

