# 21. Update the Application

Now let's deploy version 2.

Change `index.html`:

```html
<h1>Welcome to Kubernetes!</h1>
```

to:

```html
<h1>Kubernetes Web Application - Version 2</h1>
```

Also change:

```html
Version: 1.0
```

to:

```html
Version: 2.0
```

---

# 22. Build Version 2 Docker Image

Run:

```bash
docker build -t kubernetes-web-app:v2 .
```

Verify:

```bash
docker images
```

You should have:

```text
kubernetes-web-app    v1
kubernetes-web-app    v2
```

---

# 23. Update the Kubernetes Deployment

Run:

```bash
kubectl set image deployment/web-app-deployment web-container=kubernetes-web-app:v2
```

Expected:

```text
deployment.apps/web-app-deployment image updated
```

---

# 24. Monitor the Update

Run:

```bash
kubectl rollout status deployment/web-app-deployment
```

Expected:

```text
deployment "web-app-deployment" successfully rolled out
```

Kubernetes gradually replaces the old Pods with new Pods.

This is called a:

> **Rolling Update**

---

# 25. Check the New Pods

Run:

```bash
kubectl get pods
```

The Pods should be running the new version.

You can also check:

```bash
kubectl describe deployment web-app-deployment
```

Look for the container image:

```text
kubernetes-web-app:v2
```

---

# 26. Refresh the Website

Open:

```text
http://localhost:30080
```

Refresh the page.

You should now see:

```text
Kubernetes Web Application - Version 2

Version: 2.0
```

---

# 27. Check Deployment History

Run:

```bash
